{
  "slug": "rotate-array",
  "title": "Rotate Array",
  "topic": "arrays-hashing",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "pattern": "array reversal / three-reverse rotation",
  "statement": "You are given an integer array `nums` and a non-negative integer `k`. Rotate the array to the right by `k` steps and return the rotated array.\n\nRotating by one step moves the last element to the front and shifts every other element one position to the right. When `k` is larger than the length of the array, only `k` modulo `nums.length` steps count. The input array must not be modified.\n\nConstraints: `1 <= nums.length <= 200`, `-100 <= nums[i] <= 100` and `0 <= k <= 100`.\n",
  "examples": [
    {
      "input": "nums = [43], k = 25",
      "output": "[43]",
      "explanation": "Produced by the reference solution, verified against all four implementations."
    },
    {
      "input": "nums = [-100], k = 0",
      "output": "[-100]",
      "explanation": "Produced by the reference solution, verified against all four implementations."
    }
  ],
  "constraints": [
    "Function signature: `function solve(nums: number[], k: number)`.",
    "Inputs are JSON-serializable values satisfying the bounds stated above."
  ],
  "hints": [
    "The answer only depends on `k` modulo the length of the array, so reduce before doing anything else.",
    "Write down `[1,2,3,4,5,6,7]` reversed, and see which two extra reversals turn `[7,6,5,4,3,2,1]` into the rotation you want.",
    "Reverse the whole array, then the first `k` elements, then everything from index `k` onward. Check `k = 0`, `k = n` and `k > n` before you trust it."
  ],
  "editorial": {
    "approach": "The brute force performs the rotation literally: reduce `k` modulo `n`, then repeat `k` times the single-step rotation that saves the last element and shifts the rest one slot to the right. Each step is O(n), so the total is O(n\u00b7k), which is O(n\u00b2) because `k` is smaller than `n` after the reduction. The optimal uses the three-reversal identity: reversing the whole array moves the last `k` elements to the front but also flips their internal order, so reversing the first `k` elements and then the remaining `n - k` elements restores both blocks. That is two passes over the data and no per-step shifting.",
    "why_optimal": "An explicit right rotation has to move every one of the `n` elements, so \u03a9(n) is the floor, and the three-reversal method touches each element a constant number of times, giving O(n) with O(n) space for the returned copy. The brute force pays an extra factor of `k` because it re-shifts the whole array on each of the `k` steps. At `nums.length \u2264 200` both run instantly, so the linear version is worth learning for the technique, not for the judge.",
    "pitfalls": "`k = 0`: the second and third reversals must handle an empty range, so reversing `[0, k-1]` must be a no-op rather than an error. `k` greater than or equal to `n` (`k % n` first; for `k = n` or `k = 2n` the answer is the original array). `n = 1`. Reversing the wrong half first, which silently produces a left rotation. And rotating the caller's array in place when the statement asks for a returned copy, which corrupts every later test in a graded run."
  },
